---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Method v0.2"
  description="Audit the deterministic page-inventory rules, AI Fanout import boundary, evidence requirements and corrections process behind SEO Fanout."
  canonicalPath="/methodik/"
  pageClass="reference-page"
>
  <header class="reference-hero">
    <p class="mono-label">Method v0.2 · August 2026</p><h1>
      A page-decision model you can audit.
    </h1><p>
      SEO Fanout turns a keyword or user-supplied fan-out plan into one bounded
      SEO action. It does not reveal private search queries, model reasoning,
      demand, rankings or citation probability.
    </p>
  </header>
  <section class="reference-section split-section">
    <div>
      <h2>Fan-out is an input, not a URL plan</h2><p>
        Google publicly describes AI Mode using a query fan-out technique across
        subtopics and data sources. Here, a fan-out plan is only a set of
        planning branches. The current site inventory and available evidence
        determine whether those branches belong in a section, a wider page
        update, a merge, an evidence asset, one new URL or nowhere.
      </p>
    </div><div class="source-note">
      <strong>Primary public context</strong><a
        href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/search/ai-mode-search/"
        >Google: Introducing AI Mode, March 2025</a
      ><a
        href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/search/google-search-ai-mode-update/"
        >Google: AI Mode updates, May 2025</a
      ><p>
        These sources describe Google's product. They do not validate this
        site's decisions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="reference-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="mono-label">The rule path</p><h2>
        Four decisions about the site. One action.
      </h2>
    </div><div class="method-flow" aria-label="Method steps">
      <article>
        <span>01</span><h3>Bring one job</h3><p>
          Enter a keyword or import a local AI Fanout JSON plan. Imported
          branches remain user-supplied.
        </p>
      </article><article>
        <span>02</span><h3>Name the outcome</h3><p>
          Learn, compare, choose, act or troubleshoot determines useful
          questions and source roles.
        </p>
      </article><article>
        <span>03</span><h3>Inspect page ownership</h3><p>
          Distinguish no owner, a section gap, a material page gap, substantial
          coverage, overlap and an unknown inventory.
        </p>
      </article><article>
        <span>04</span><h3>Check evidence readiness</h3><p>
          None and secondary-only evidence block publication actions. Primary,
          firsthand or reproducible evidence can support them.
        </p>
      </article><article>
        <span>05</span><h3>Apply one rule</h3><p>
          The deterministic engine returns exactly one of six page actions.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="reference-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="mono-label">Decision table</p><h2>The exact v0.2 rule set</h2>
    </div><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" aria-label="Method decision table">
      <table>
        <thead
          ><tr><th>Inventory state</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Decision</th></tr
          ></thead
        ><tbody
          ><tr
            ><td>Several pages overlap</td><td>Any</td><td
              >Merge overlapping content</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>One bounded section is missing</td><td>Primary or observed</td
            ><td>Add one section</td></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>One bounded section is missing</td><td>None or secondary</td
            ><td>Build an evidence asset first</td></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>The page promise needs material expansion</td><td
              >Primary or observed</td
            ><td>Extend the existing page</td></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>The page promise needs material expansion</td><td
              >None or secondary</td
            ><td>Build an evidence asset first</td></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>Page substantially covers the job</td><td
              >Primary or observed</td
            ><td>Take no content action</td></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>Page substantially covers the job</td><td>None or secondary</td
            ><td>Build an evidence asset first</td></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>No page owns the job</td><td>Primary or observed</td><td
              >Create one new URL</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>No page owns the job</td><td>None or secondary</td><td
              >Build an evidence asset first</td
            ></tr
          ><tr
            ><td>Inventory unknown</td><td>Any</td><td
              >Build an evidence asset first</td
            ></tr
          ></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="reference-section split-section" id="privacy">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label">Local import & retention</p><h2>
        Your plan stays in the tab.
      </h2><p>
        Pasted and selected JSON is parsed in browser memory. There is no
        upload, model call, account, analytics, cookie or browser-storage write.
        Local copy and export happen only after direct action.
      </p>
    </div><div>
      <p class="mono-label">Uncertainty</p><h2>
        Deterministic does not mean automatically correct.
      </h2><p>
        The result follows declared inputs. Inspect real URLs, current primary
        sources, rights, user evidence and business constraints before acting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="reference-section split-section" id="decision-stress-test">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label">Decision Stress Test</p><h2>
        Change the inventory assumption, not the provider.
      </h2><p>
        The lab holds evidence constant and varies page coverage so you can see
        which unverified site assumption flips the action.
      </p>
    </div><div>
      <p class="mono-label">Separate upstream role</p><h2>
        AI Fanout maps plausible branches.
      </h2><p>
        AI Fanout is the separate upstream planner and public-answer evidence
        workspace. It shares the same operator and is not independent
        validation.
      </p><a class="text-link" href="/lab/"
        >Open the SEO stress test <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="reference-section split-section" id="ownership">
    <div>
      <p class="mono-label">Operator and author</p><h2>
        Matthias Ramahi is accountable.
      </h2><p>
        Matthias Ramahi operates and authors SEO Fanout. AI Fanout and
        Contextter are related products under the same operator, not independent
        sources or validators.
      </p>
    </div><div>
      <p class="mono-label">Corrections</p><h2>
        The framework is versioned.
      </h2><p>
        Report unclear rules, source descriptions, accessibility problems or
        reproducibility failures through the published correction route.
      </p><a class="text-link" href="/korrekturen/"
        >Read the correction SLA <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
